# soc_small.buda -- the SoC vehicle at NQ=8, as a flat script.
#
# GENERATED.  Do not hand-edit: the design lives in flow/tcl/soc.tcl and this
# is a RECORDING of one run of it.  Regenerate with
#
#     tools/tcl2buda.py flow/tcl/soc.tcl 8 -o flow/soc_small.buda
#
# and add --verify to have the replay's measured result compared against the
# Tcl run's (identical on abstract WL, detailed WL, bit-wires and overlaps
# when this was written).
#
# It is a FLATTENING, not a parameterization: one concrete design with the
# loops unrolled and the branches resolved, so there is no dial in here.  For
# any other size, shape or flag (-bottomup, -caps, -bydepth, -NC, -DW ...) run
# soc.tcl itself -- see flow/tcl/soc.md.
#
# Measured on the container this was recorded on (~3 s, `bin/buda --no-viz`):
#   323 bundles / 2023 candidates / 9328 bit-wires
#   detailed WL 1,968,672, ending clean -- 0 overlaps / 0 unplaced / 0 violations.
# Read those as a scale, not a target; another box will differ.
#
# The design: eleven leaf cell types repeating a different number of times
# each, leaves 2-4 levels down depending on subsystem, routed hierarchically
# (run_hier_bundler / generate_hier_topologies / run_planner hier).
#
# A recording holds only the commands that RAN, so the two differ in what
# they cover: here the first `check_design dnuts` comes back DIRTY (40
# violations in 2 bundles), soc.tcl's heal_if_dirty fires, and the
# `negotiate_congestion` + `ripup_reroute` lines below are that round -- so
# this vehicle exercises the healers.  flow/soc_mid.buda does not: it is
# clean at the first audit and records no healer at all.

# origin: 
def_layer 2 M2 H 55.56
